From: panvk-kbase-android worker <panvk-worker@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH perf 006] panvk/csf: destroy retired tiler heaps on the heap worker

KBASE_IOCTL_CS_TILER_HEAP_TERM takes ~3.2 ms per call on the submitting
thread and was nearly the entire tiler-heap renewal cost (~20.7 ms/s of
queue_submit time in on-device profiling).  The ioctl only frees the heap
chunks; nothing in the submit path waits for it to finish.  The seqno
check in kbase_try_destroy_retired_heap() is what proves the firmware no
longer references the retired heap, and that check runs before the
descriptor slot is recycled.

Hand the already-proven-safe heap contexts to the existing panvk_heap
worker through a small SPSC ring, and only fall back to an inline TERM if
the worker is unavailable or the ring is full (neither happens in
practice: at most PANVK_KBASE_HEAP_SLOTS - 1 = 3 contexts are retired).

- kbase_destroy_heaps_job() pops and destroys contexts, then re-checks the
  ring after clearing job_pending so a push that raced the flag cannot be
  lost.
- kbase_destroy_heaps_kick() deduplicates queued jobs with an atomic
  exchange; the destroy job shares the single panvk_heap thread with heap
  pre-creation (util_queue max_jobs = 2, one kick of each kind at a time).
- kbase_spare_heap_finish() drains the ring after util_queue_destroy(),
  because u_queue drops queued jobs without running them.
- The destroy no longer runs in kbase_try_destroy_retired_heap(), so its
  local struct panvk_device is gone.

The kernel still owns the heap chunks and frees them exactly as before,
just on the worker thread.  Deferring the TERM can only delay chunk/VA
reuse, never let the GPU see a freed heap early.

Purpose: take the ~3.2 ms TERM ioctl off the CPU submit thread.
Tested GPU: build only, not yet run on device.
Tested Kbase UAPI: CSF 1.21.
Mesa base range: 26.3.0-devel (pinned 5a07217f).
Dependencies: csf/007 (creates this file); no overlap with perf/001.
Validation test: TBD, on-device render smoke test.

Generated-by: LLM

